{
  "_comment": [
    "International dial code -> ISO 3166-1 alpha-2, for the OnlineSim adapter.",
    "",
    "Why this file exists: OnlineSim identifies countries by dial code (7, 233,",
    "373), while our Country model stores ISO alpha-2 and is unique on it. The",
    "two have to be reconciled somewhere, and a lookup table that can be read and",
    "corrected is better than a guess buried in code.",
    "",
    "The rule the adapter follows: a dial code that is not in this table is",
    "SKIPPED, with a warning, not guessed at. A wrong mapping would sell a",
    "customer a number from the wrong country, which is worse than not offering",
    "it at all.",
    "",
    "Ambiguous codes are deliberately omitted rather than resolved arbitrarily.",
    "+1 covers the USA, Canada and around twenty Caribbean territories; +7 covers",
    "Russia and Kazakhstan; +44 covers the UK plus Crown dependencies. Where a",
    "code is shared, only the assignment OnlineSim itself labels is usable, so",
    "those are listed under 'ambiguous' and resolved by name instead.",
    "",
    "Source: ITU-T E.164 country calling codes. Reference data, not invented."
  ],

